sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: stage 2 of the command development pipeline. A task VM, where an agent or Nick develops a command and the proof cases run too, holds a nested fleet (a Gateway with Swamp, an app-dev Node, an app-prod Node) that resets from the ready copies in the host's topology seed. The nested VMs hold their own checkout; orbit-nested syncs the author's changes into them, so memory snapshots work. Uses the simple slot pool (task-vm.slots) as the target way to get the VM.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: development pipeline stage 2 · development environment
  %% kind: pipeline.2-development-environment
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane H icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane VM icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  %% lane NT icon=server pill=fleet tone=gray
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>provision run
  participant H as Task VM host<br/>Incus over ssh
  participant VM as Task VM<br/>outer · operator
  participant NT as Nested fleet<br/>Gateway · 2 Nodes
  Note over GW: Admit the task<br/>group at a commit
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - an external task group in `todo` and a free task VM host
  %%> Result:
  %%> - the full intent is recorded; the group is reserved
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>tasks provision
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the same run for a person, an agent or a CI proof
  SW->>H: Take a task VM<br/>ready slot, else clone
  %%> Target, not built:
  %%> - start a ready slot that was enrolled ahead of time and kept stopped; takes seconds (chart `task-vm.slots`)
  %%> Built today:
  %%> - clone the task image, boot, enrol (keys, WireGuard, observer), model key, Pi; takes minutes
  SW->>H: Attach the topology seed<br/>clone under the host lock
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - clones the seed of the host's task image; on ZFS the clone costs only what the Nodes write
  %%> - the VM sees it as the disk `topology-seed`
  %%> Why:
  %%> - each host keeps its own seed; nothing is copied between hosts
  SW->>VM: Check out the commit<br/>git fetch · task token
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - checks out the commit or branch the task works on, with a read token from the vault
  SW->>VM: Reset the nested fleet<br/>from the ready copies
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - per Node: copies the ready copy, adds the saved NIC, starts all three
  %%> - no code share from the outer VM: each ready copy holds its own checkout with `vendor` and `node_modules` at the seed's commit
  %%> Time:
  %%> - about 75 seconds to a checked fleet, of which the boot is about 50
  opt no usable ready copies
    VM->>NT: Build the fleet<br/>then make ready copies
    %%> When:
    %%> - a new task image has a seed with only the Node template until its first seed refresh (stage 6)
    %%> Time:
    %%> - about 230 seconds; the build ends by making the ready copies for the next reset
  end
  VM->>NT: Boot the fleet<br/>real nested WireGuard
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the three Nodes share a real nested network; the outer VM is the operator
  SW->>NT: Sync the task's code<br/>same sync as orbit-nested
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - rsync of the outer checkout into the nested Gateway and app-dev, only what differs from the seed's commit
  %%> - leaves out `vendor`, `node_modules`, `.git`, `.env`, `storage` and `bootstrap/cache`
  %%> - `composer install` or `bun install` only when a lock file changed, from the cache in the VM; then migrations and a Swamp reload
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the seed is refreshed after each release, so the difference stays small
  opt playbooks or pins changed, also after a rebase
    SW->>VM: Converge once<br/>outer VM · nested Nodes
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - image playbooks on the outer VM; role playbooks and pins on the nested Nodes, through the nested Orbit's `node:converge`
    %%> - runs after the sync, so the nested Orbit has the new playbooks
    %%> - after a rebase onto main, a running task picks up the tools main added since its start
    %%> Why:
    %%> - a tool update lives in code; this puts it in place once, and the snapshot taken next keeps it
    %%> - the base images are not touched; only the merge and the seed refresh change them
  end
  VM->>NT: Check readiness<br/>agents · Swamp · Doctor
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - every Node agent online, the Swamp worker enrolled, the Gateway knows the operator, Doctor has no finding
  SW->>NT: Take the base snapshot<br/>memory included
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - one Incus stateful snapshot `base` of the three nested VMs (`migration.stateful`, `size.state` = memory)
  %%> - every proof batch starts from it (stage 3)
  %%> - holds the proof Swamp Club key, pushed in after the restore of the ready copies; accepted because `base` belongs to this task VM only and is destroyed with it; ready copies stay key-free
  %%> Why:
  %%> - possible because no VM mounts a share from the outer VM
  SW-->>GW: Environment ready<br/>terminal event
  %%> Result:
  %%> - the Gateway marks the group running and records the VM; the proof (stage 3) or the proof CI (stage 4) uses the fleet
  loop while the author develops
    VM->>NT: Every orbit-nested call<br/>syncs changes first
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - `orbit-nested` on the outer VM runs the same sync before each call; well under a second when nothing changed
    %%> - `orbit-nested exec NODE -- CMD` does the same for direct commands, such as `artisan`; the task brief says to use it
    %%> - when the change holds playbooks or pins, or after a rebase onto main, the sync also runs the converge step above once
    %%> Why:
    %%> - the agent never has to remember a sync; edits reach the fleet like the old share
  end
  opt the author wants a clean fleet
    SW->>NT: Restore the base snapshot<br/>about 16 s
    %%> What it keeps:
    %%> - the outer VM, Pi and the checkout; the next orbit-nested call syncs the latest code again
  end
  SW->>H: Destroy the task VM<br/>always · success or failure
  %%> Why:
  %%> - no proof VM stays behind; diagnostics are kept, not running VMs
  %%> Target, not built:
  %%> - the Gateway then enrols a new slot to refill the pool of ready slots
